  - P1-1 (i) and (ii) through `(abandon)`;
  - `(trusted)` prints own plus trusted;
  - `(reset)` drops the trusted set unless given in `create-state`.

P1-1, retirement:

- (i) A session with absent or zero counters: the first require mints
  `[:t0 0]` and opens an unanswered admission; `(abandon)`; an unrelated
  trusted require completes on the same pair.
- (ii) A nonzero base: two trusted requires first, then a run whose
  module installs a child that requires another untrusted module
  (origins `:t0.<n>` and `:t0.<n>.0`); abandon. `:retired` holds exactly
  the run-owned requests still encountered from the current cursor
  before the first unrelated request; no run-owned admission remains in
  `:asked` or `:answers`; an unrelated trusted require completes. Root
  retirement is exercised separately in the unanswered-root case (i).
- (iii) `retire` over a pair holding requests from `:t0.1`, `:t0.1.0` and
  `:t0.10` with `:children [1 2]` retires the first two and stops at the
  `:t0.10` request.

P1-2, the drain:

- (i) After the initial drive stops, append `serve-budget` answers for
  unknown ids and then the valid allow; call `recheck-on-tick` alone, at
  most twice: the require completes, and `:checks` is 0 after the first
  call.
- (ii) Gap recovery: a pair built with capacity 4 (`make-pair` takes an
  optional capacity, default `pair-capacity`); mint the cursor at the
  oldest end, append six answers for unknown ids and then the valid
  allow; the drain adopts the recovery cursor on the gap outcome and the
  require completes within two calls.
- (iii) `serve-budget` irrelevant answers and no allow, three hook calls:
  `:checks` stays 0 and the cursor has advanced past all of them.

## 8. Evaluation

### 8.1 Entry

Loaded code enters a session by the flow of `yin.vm.linker.md` 7.2 and 7.3,
unchanged: the require parks on the link pair; the interpreter answers under
the request's id; the receiving task correlates, discharges obligations
against its own live state (step 5b), runs the install child, and lowers its
own bindings. This holds on all four VMs: each kernel names its format and
contract through `IModuleKernel/link-format`, and the `:fallback` policy of
`yin.vm.linker.md` 5.5 applies as composed.

### 8.2 Pending and the re-check (R11)

- A link the DHT source answers pending records, on the shell's pending
  run, the module manifest address whose load it waits on.
- `yin.repl.main/step-all` calls `yin.repl/recheck-pending` once in a tick
  if and only if that tick's node events hold a `:loaded` or `:load-failed`
  event whose `:manifest` is one a pending link waits on.
- Any other node event (a bind, a publication settling, an index load,
  another module's load) triggers no re-check and changes no `:checks`.
- A terminal load event arrives once. A re-check after it finds the record
  terminal and answers the link; a second re-check for the same address
  cannot happen without a new load.
- One re-check serves every pending request in order, so several pending
  module loads completing in one tick need one re-check.
- Beside it, `step-all` calls the AD-1 hook `yin.repl/recheck-on-answers`,
  which drains the admission response medium and re-checks once if and
  only if a drained answer's admission id is one a pending link waits on.
  An irrelevant answer changes no `:checks`. AL-2 replaces both hooks with

